    so that the "score focused" could be brought
    back into contact with reality "with a thump",

    (2) the player achieving that score, then
    deliberately arranged, after rolling the score
    counter, to be killed by a _newt_, just to drive
    home with a mallet the point of how silly "score
    bragging" is, and

    (3) those points were _not_ scored "in the
    midgame", but in the endgame, by killing
    Pestilence in a loop using a pasted keystroke
    macro to repeat the actions "zillions" of times
    after creating a set of constraints so that only
    repeated, predictable motions by all monsters
    in Astral were possible.

Like many other things the "score focused" find
objectionable in other players' NetHack strategies,
it was a patience-tactics game, but one set up
specifically when and where it was to take advantage
of the high score per death available from killing
one of the Riders.

In some sense then, "fixing" the scoring system to
disallow such scores is merely an attempt to ignore
the point made by that player, and will serve as a
way of caving in to pressures from the
score-obsessed to make scores meaningful again.

Still, fixing the scoring to make repeated "no
increased danger" playing tactics receive less
reward for each repetition is a huge improvement
over the alternative proposals, by people incapable
of patience-tactics playing, to _disable_
credit-cloning, polypiling, pudding farming,
alchemy, extinctionism, stash management, and other
"high risk for repetitive motion injury" tactics
that for some of us count as "fun".

Personally, I'd much rather pudding farming did
_not_ rapidly drive up my XL, that isn't my goal in
doing it, I do it for the death dropped "stuff" and
the sacrifice goodies, and I don't _want_ the added
XP, so cutting down both the score benefits and the
XP benefits of patience-tactics play would suit me
to a "T".

As a suggestion, many places in NetHack, what would
usually be computations with real numbers are
instead done by using a mix of integer arithmetic
and random applications of that arithmetic, so that
any real number decrementing multiplier can be
pretty closely approximated. It shouldn't be too
hard to arrange that each kill of a particular
monster decrease the score value for that monster,
until after some Nth kill of that type, where the
setting for N might vary from, say, 5 to 127
depending on your attitude toward extinctionism
versus pudding farming, the score bottomed out at a
single point per monster of that type killed. After
that, if someone wants to try to roll the score
counter at a single point per kill for over a
billion iterations, I suggest we let them.

